.TH WB\-STATUS 1 "" "watchbill"
.SH NAME
wb\-status \- list the issues in the flow and where each stands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwb\fR status [<issue>] [\-\-stage <word>] [\-s | \-\-short | \-\-porcelain] [\-\-color[=<when>]] [\-q | \-\-quiet]
.br
or \-\-session <hash> [\-q | \-\-quiet]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
List the jobs in the flow as git status lists files, under two headings by whose move it is next: Jobs waiting for you (a question is open, a session ended needing a person, an answer waits for a person, or the plan is to be accepted) and Jobs in the flow (an agent's move). A line is the number, the branch, the open change as #<n> (change_of_branch) and a text: the stage word (planned, accepted, tested, built, reviewed, fixed), how many questions are open, the reason of a needs_person, and how many commits are not pushed. A job not broken down has no line, unless a person's move is open on it. Hint lines in git's form, as (use "wb answer <issue>..." to answer its questions), come under the first heading, and under a kept view of one issue, unless status_hints: false is written in watchbill.yml. \-s (\-\-short) prints one line for each job, as git status \-s does: the stage letter (P A T B R F), the letter of why a person must move (Q a question, N a needs_person, W an answer, P a plan to accept), the number and the branch; \-\-porcelain prints the same with no colour, for a script. \-\-color[=<when>] (always, never or auto, the default: only on a terminal) colours the lines under the first heading red and those under the second green, as git colours what is not staged and what is. An issue is in the flow when its branch, local or at origin, holds a commit that the default branch of origin does not: git alone says which, and fetches nothing.
.PP
Only true and false are values of status_hints. A job that could not be read is ?? and its number in the short form, and its reason goes to standard error. \-\-porcelain never colours and never prints a hint, whatever \-\-color says; given with \-\-short it wins, as in git. \-\-color alone is \-\-color=always. The hints are not coloured, nor are the headings.
.PP
The stamps are the commits on the job's ref that count. The questions and answers of a job whose ref holds a stamp, a commit of wb plan or a questions commit are read from the ref alone, from the first of them on, and no issue_comments is run for it, so a project with no issue_comments reads it; those of a job begun before, whose ref holds none of them, come from issue_comments. For an open question read from the ref it says on standard error that an answer typed at the tracker is not read. An acceptance that a source or a planner round after it voided puts the job back to planned. For one issue it says source changed where issue_text, the pattern that read the newest source of its plan, prints other text now: a person runs wb plan add again, and wb status takes nothing in. A source was read by it where its source is the pattern with the issue filled, as \-\-from writes it, or as \-\-source gives it. The list runs no issue_text. An acceptance counts only where its signed tag checks against the keys of .watchbill/signers of the default branch with the role accept, and a stamp only where it is signed by a key there with start; <issue> says under Worked out from by whom it is signed, or why it counts for nothing (the tag not there, unsigned, a key not in the list, a key without accept, a signature that does not check, another job, another repository: a base that is no commit here, or a job that is not the ref's). Where the default branch holds no list, or one with no key line, or there is none, nothing that needs a signature counts, and <issue> says once: no .watchbill/signers on <branch>: nothing is signed, so nothing counts. Where the job's ref is not as wb writes it (one line of commits, with no merge, each signed by a key of the list with the role it needs, and each stamp's tree its parent's but for view and issue.yml), nothing on it counts, the acceptance counts as void, and <issue> names the first commit that is not, on the line of first parents, on one line. A job whose last session ended needing a person, written as a signed commit on its ref, says needs a person: <reason> on its line, and <issue> prints waits for: person (<reason>); it stays until the next start of any role on the issue, and a questions commit, an answers commit, a flag or a comment does not close it. <issue> prints that issue, in the flow or not, with its title (issue_address), each session (its stamp, how it ended, what came back) and what it was worked out from; \-\-stage <word> prints the jobs of that stage word only (or not read), whatever its case. A job whose work is accepted, or whose change is merged, is done: it is not listed, and <issue> prints Done and what closed it, and a line accepted: for each acceptance, by its object. For one issue it also prints a line kept: <role> round <n>, <k> commits not taken, <folder> for each view kept with work not taken back, and cut off: <role> round <n>, <folder> for each session whose wb ended before it did, and running: <role> round <n>, <run time>, quiet <quiet time> for each session that runs, its wb alive, as 'running: build round 2, 7h12m, quiet 6h50m'. With no issue it says on standard error how many such views are kept on this machine. A stamp of the tester, the build or the fix puts an issue at its step only where a commit of its round came back, as the lines of the starts on this machine say; without them the stamp counts. For one issue it prints <role> round <n>: no commit came back for each round that brought nothing back, and goes on with kept: <what it holds>, <folder> where a view is kept for it. A view of over 200 commits is said as the first 200 and 'and <k> more', and each view is read within 20 seconds. Ends with 0 when every issue could be read, 1 when a pattern failed for one (it is listed under Not read), 2 when it was used wrongly or the repository is not set up.
.PP
Writes nothing but a temporary repository in $TMPDIR, removed after, where it reads a kept view.
.PP
\-\-session <hash>, with no issue and no \-\-stage, prints one session from git alone: the lines of its stamp, Base: the commit it was made from, the commits that a note names for it, newest last, Last: the newest of them, and Ended: how its start ended where this machine holds the lines of the start, else 'not known on this machine'. <hash> is the commit of a stamp that counts, on a job's ref, or a unique start of it of 12 or more hex digits. A note is not signed and counts for nothing: it is only where the commits are found. A hash that names no stamp that counts, or not one only, ends with 1, and fewer than 12 digits with 2. It only reads: it writes nothing and asks nothing of the network.
.PP
\-q (\-\-quiet) is taken and changes nothing: what it prints is what a script reads.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B <issue>
Print that issue, in the flow or not, with its title (issue_address), its sessions and what it was worked out from.
.TP
.B \-\-stage <word>
Print the jobs of that stage word only (planned, accepted, tested, built, reviewed, fixed, or not read), whatever its case.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-short
Print one line for each job, as git status \-s does.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print the same lines with no colour, for a script.
.TP
.B \-\-color
Colour the lines: \-\-color=always, \-\-color=never or \-\-color=auto (the default: only on a terminal); alone it is always.
.TP
.B \-\-session <hash>
Print one session from git alone: its stamp, its base, the commits that a note names for it, and how its start ended where this machine holds the lines of it. No issue and no \-\-stage with it.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Taken, and changes nothing: what the command prints is what a script reads.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every issue could be read. With \-\-session: the session is printed.
.TP
.B 1
A pattern failed for one issue. It is listed under Not read. With \-\-session: the hash names no stamp that counts, or not one only, or the notes cannot be read.
.TP
.B 2
It was used wrongly or the repository is not set up.
.SH SEE ALSO
